 would signal the race is tightening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Gus Bilirakis (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Gus Bilirakis seeks another term in a Tampa-area district that leans Republican by about seven points. He has raised nearly $1.6 million and holds a large cash advantage, while Democratic nominee Kimberly Overman raised under $200,000 and had almost nothing banked after her August primary win. Ratings from nine outlets split between Solid R and Likely R, and prediction markets give Democrats only about a one-in-ten chance.\n\nThere are signs of Democratic interest: the House campaign arm listed the district as in play, and a June Democratic-sponsored poll showed Bilirakis at just 42 percent. Still, with no credible general-election polling and Overman's financial weakness, the race remains clearly tilted toward the incumbent, with a small chance of an upset if the national environment breaks hard against Republicans.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0813, "margin": -8.73}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0907, "margin": -9.5}]}